.TH "CLOSEWATCHER.REQUESTCLOSE" "3JS" "March 24, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
CloseWatcher.requestClose \- CloseWatcher: requestClose() method
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[B]\f[CB]requestClose()\f[B]\f[R] method of the
\f[CR]CloseWatcher\f[R] interface fires a \f[CR]cancel\f[R] event and if
that event is not canceled with \f[CR]Event.preventDefault()\f[R],
proceeds to fire a \f[CR]close\f[R] event, and then finally deactivates
the close watcher as if \f[CR]destroy()\f[R] was called.
.SH SYNTAX
.IP
.EX
requestClose()
.EE
.SS Parameters
None.
.SS Return value
None (\f[CR]undefined\f[R]).
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Using the \f[CR]requestClose()\f[R] method
In this example, you have your own UI component (a picker), and you want
to support both the platform\(cqs default close method (e.g., the Esc
key) and your custom close method (a close button).
.PP
The \f[CR]onclick\f[R] handler of your UI component can call
\f[CR]requestClose\f[R] to request a close and to route your close
request through the same \f[CR]onclose\f[R] handler the platform close
method uses.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] watcher = \f[B]new\f[R] CloseWatcher();
\f[B]const\f[R] picker = setUpAndShowPickerDOMElement();
\f[B]let\f[R] chosenValue = \f[B]null\f[R];

watcher.onclose = () \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  chosenValue = picker.querySelector(\(dqinput\(dq).value;
  picker.remove();
};

picker.querySelector(\(dq.close\-button\(dq).onclick = () \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  watcher.requestClose();
};
.EE
